---
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../components/SiteHeader.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../components/SiteFooter.astro';
import SectionNav from '../components/SectionNav.astro';
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro';
import InstallTabs from '../components/InstallTabs.astro';
import CopyableCode from '../components/CopyableCode.astro';
import lejiSyntaxTheme from '../styles/shiki-leji.json';
// The badge shown below is the canonical fixture itself, not a copy: the four
// files under fixtures/badge/ are the byte oracle the three SDKs are tested
// against, so the sample on this page cannot drift from what `leji badge` writes.
// It carries its own role="img", <title> and aria-label, so it needs no wrapper.
import badgeSvg from '../../../../fixtures/badge/governed.svg?raw';

// The badge markdown exactly as `leji badge` prints it, for one level, so the
// sample and the tool cannot read differently: the shape is
// packages/sdk/src/commands/badge.ts (`badgeMarkdown`, `badgeLabel`), and the
// four canonical lines are fixtures/badge/<level>.md.
const badgeLine = '[![Leji 1.0 · governed · self-attested](leji-badge.svg)](https://leji.org/agent-ready/)';
---

<Base
   title="Agent-ready · Leji"
   description="Install the Leji CLI and run leji start: your agent boots from the team's context layer. Also what the self-attested conformance badge claims, and how it is generated."
>
   <SiteHeader />
   <div class="wrap">
      <div class="doc-shell">
         <SectionNav current={Astro.url.pathname} />
         <main id="content" class="agent-ready-main measured">
            <section class="hero">
               <PageHeader kind="guide" />
               <h1>Your repo, agent-ready in two commands.</h1>
               <p class="lede">
                  The badge that most likely brought you here marks a repository whose context layer an agent can boot
                  from, validated and scored on the maintainer's own machine and self-attested. Making your own repo
                  agent-ready takes the two commands below.
               </p>
               <p class="jump"><a href="#badge">What the badge attests ↓</a></p>
            </section>

            <section class="cmds" aria-label="The two commands">
               <div class="cmd">
                  <p class="cmd-label">01 · Install the CLI</p>
                  <div class="cmd-install"><InstallTabs /></div>
               </div>
               <div class="cmd">
                  <p class="cmd-label">02 · Enter the context layer</p>
                  <CopyableCode code="leji start" lang="bash" label="Copy the start command" />
               </div>
            </section>

            <p class="fine">
               No context layer in the repository yet? The <a href="/quickstart/">quickstart</a> walks the fuller path, from
               adopting an existing repo to scoring the level it claims.
            </p>

            <section class="what">
               <h2>What just happened</h2>
               <ul>
                  <li>
                     Your agent opened in the context layer and read the <a href="/spec/boot-profile/">boot profile</a> first,
                     so its first context is the team's rather than whatever it inferred from the tree.
                  </li>
                  <li>
                     Validation and <a href="/spec/conformance/">conformance scoring</a> run from the package you just installed,
                     on your own machine, with no service in the loop.
                  </li>
                  <li>
                     The tooling opens no connection of its own, and there is no account, no registry and no hosted
                     service behind it. What reaches the network, you invoke yourself: the federation fetches contact
                     the repository you named, and the dependency step at <code>leji init</code> runs your own package manager
                     on your explicit yes. The agent leji launches is the one you already run, with its own network behavior.
                  </li>
               </ul>
            </section>

            <section class="badge" id="badge">
               <h2>What the badge attests</h2>
               <p>
                  The badge is generated locally: <code>leji badge</code> scores the repository with <code
                     >leji conformance</code
                  >, writes the SVG for the level that offline run verified, and prints one markdown line that embeds
                  it.
               </p>
               <CopyableCode code="leji badge" lang="bash" label="Copy the badge command" />
               <p class="caption">
                  For a context layer verified at the <code>governed</code> level, the badge is this:
               </p>
               <div class="badge-shot" set:html={badgeSvg} />
               <aside class="attest-note">
                  <strong>Self-attested</strong> means scored by the repository's own tooling: no registry, no endpoint, no
                  account. The badge states the level this offline run verified, never more than the context layer claims
                  and possibly less.
               </aside>
               <p class="caption">The markdown line that embeds it in a README:</p>
               <div class="badge-md"><Code code={badgeLine} lang="markdown" theme={lejiSyntaxTheme} wrap /></div>
               <p>
                  The levels are <code>core</code>, <code>indexed</code>, <code>governed</code> and <code
                     >federated</code
                  >, each containing the one before it. <a href="/spec/conformance/">The conformance checklist</a> says what
                  each level asks of a team, and every one of them is reachable with the open specification and the reference
                  tooling alone.
               </p>
               <p>
                  So the thing to read is the check, not the picture: run <code>leji conformance</code> in the same repository
                  and see what it reports. <a href="/trust/">Trust</a> covers who maintains Leji, what the 1.0 line promises,
                  and what happens if the steward disappears.
               </p>
            </section>
         </main>
      </div>
   </div>
   <SiteFooter />
</Base>

<style>
   /* No top padding: .doc-shell already sets the space above the first line, and
      every doc page starts its h1 there. */
   .hero {
      padding: 0 0 1rem;
      max-width: 760px;
   }
   .lede {
      font-size: var(--text-lede);
      line-height: 1.7;
      color: var(--leji-text-body);
      max-width: var(--measure-fine);
      margin: 0;
   }

   h2 {
      font-family: var(--serif-display);
      font-weight: 560;
      font-size: 1.6rem;
      line-height: 1.25;
      color: var(--leji-text);
      margin: 0 0 0.6rem;
   }

   p,
   li {
      color: var(--leji-text-body);
   }
   a {
      color: var(--leji-link);
      text-decoration-color: var(--leji-brand);
      text-underline-offset: 3px;
   }
   code {
      background: var(--leji-code-bg);
      padding: 0.1em 0.35em;
      border-radius: 4px;
      font-size: 0.85em;
      color: var(--leji-text);
   }

   /* The jump the badge-clicker takes when the attestation is what they came for.
      Same instrument as the quickstart's "what each level asks of you" link. */
   .jump {
      margin: 1.1rem 0 0;
   }
   .jump a {
      font-family: var(--mono);
      font-size: 0.74rem;
      letter-spacing: 0.06em;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      text-decoration: none;
      border-bottom: 1px solid var(--leji-brand);
      padding-bottom: 2px;
      white-space: nowrap;
   }
   .jump a:hover {
      color: var(--leji-text);
      border-color: var(--leji-text);
   }

   /* The pitch: the two commands, one under the other, nothing between them. */
   .cmds {
      margin-top: 1.8rem;
      display: grid;
      gap: 1.8rem;
   }
   .cmd-label {
      font-family: var(--mono);
      font-size: 0.74rem;
      letter-spacing: 0.06em;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      color: var(--leji-text-body);
      margin: 0 0 0.6rem;
   }
   .cmd-install {
      background: var(--leji-deep);
      border-radius: 12px;
      box-shadow: 0 14px 40px rgba(24, 61, 59, 0.16);
      overflow: hidden;
   }

   .fine {
      margin: 1.6rem 0 0;
   }

   .what {
      margin-top: 3.4rem;
   }
   .what ul {
      margin: 0.6rem 0 0;
      padding-left: 1.1rem;
   }
   .what li {
      margin: 0.5rem 0;
   }

   .badge {
      margin-top: 3.4rem;
      padding-bottom: 1rem;
      /* the jump link lands the heading below the sticky header, not under it */
      scroll-margin-top: calc(var(--header-h) + 1.5rem);
   }
   .badge p {
      margin: 0 0 1.1rem;
   }
   .badge .caption {
      margin: 1.4rem 0 0;
   }
   /* The badge at its own size on the page background, which is how it sits in a
      README: no frame, no scaling, nothing this page adds to it. The face shows the
      level alone, so the note under it carries what the badge claims. */
   .badge-shot {
      margin: 0.9rem 0 1rem;
   }
   .badge-shot :global(svg) {
      display: block;
   }

   /* Same instrument as the quickstart's map-note: brand rule, accent ground. Kept
      at body size rather than that page's smaller setting, because this is the
      section's claim and not marginalia. */
   .attest-note {
      margin: 0 0 1.4rem;
      padding: 0.9rem 1.2rem;
      border-left: 3px solid var(--leji-brand);
      background: var(--leji-accent-soft);
      border-radius: 0 8px 8px 0;
      color: var(--leji-text-body);
      line-height: 1.6;
   }
   .attest-note strong {
      color: var(--leji-text);
   }

   /* The printed markdown is a sample, not a line to copy: one level's wording,
      whichever level the reader's own run verifies. Hence no copy button. */
   .badge-md {
      margin: 0.6rem 0 1.6rem;
   }
   .badge-md :global(.astro-code) {
      background: var(--leji-deep) !important;
      border-radius: 10px;
      padding: 1rem 1.2rem;
      font-size: var(--code-fz);
      line-height: 1.6;
      margin: 0;
      overflow-x: auto;
   }
</style>
